course: history
lesson: 70
title: Trade: why people swap, and the great trade routes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mei's friend says the Silk Road was one long road like a motorway. Charlie reads that pepper was worth its weight in gold. Amina from Mali says Mansa Musa was the richest man ever, innit. Some of this is true, some is stretched.
explain: What really happened: the Silk Road was a web of many routes, few traders went the whole way, and goods passed from hand to hand; even the name was made up in 1877. Spices were dear in medieval Europe but not usually worth as much as gold. Mansa Musa really did carry so much gold to Cairo in 1324 that its price there reportedly fell.
explain: Think of Josh and Leo swapping football stickers: Josh has loads of Messi, Leo has loads of Ronaldo, and after the swap both are happier. That is the heart of trade: both sides can win.
explain: Trade is older than cities. Sharp volcanic glass called obsidian travelled hundreds of kilometres over 10,000 years ago. Later, Indian Ocean sailors rode the monsoon winds, which Hassan's uncle from Kenya calls «proper clever», and merchant towns like Lübeck joined the Hanseatic League.
explain: Today most goods cross the sea in steel boxes all the same size, and one T-shirt may grow in India and be sewn in Bangladesh. Labels like Fairtrade try to give farmers fairer prices, though how much they help is debated.
fact: Obsidian, a sharp volcanic glass, was traded over long distances more than 10,000 years ago, before the first cities.
fact: The Silk Road was many routes linking China, Central Asia, the Middle East and Europe; few traders went the whole way, and the name was made up in 1877 by a German geographer.
fact: Mansa Musa ruled the Mali Empire; on his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 he gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value there reportedly fell.
fact: West Africa traded gold for salt across the Sahara by camel, and cities like Timbuktu became rich centres of learning.
fact: Indian Ocean sailors timed their voyages with the seasonal monsoon winds, linking East Africa, Arabia, India and Southeast Asia; Swahili cities like Kilwa and Mombasa grew rich.
fact: From the 1200s the Hanseatic League linked trading towns around the Baltic and North Seas, such as Lübeck, Bergen and Novgorod, trading fish, furs and grain.
fact: The Grand Bazaar in Istanbul was started in the 1450s and is one of the oldest and biggest covered markets in the world.
fact: Spices were expensive in medieval Europe but not usually worth their weight in gold, and they were not used to hide rotten meat.
fact: In 1817 the economist David Ricardo explained that countries gain by doing what they do best and trading, like swapping spare stickers.
fact: In 1956 Malcom McLean started using standard shipping containers; today about four in five tonnes of world trade go by sea.
fact: Many things are made in several countries: cotton grown in one, cloth made in another, clothes sewn in a third and sold in a fourth.
fact: Fairtrade labels began in the late 1980s to promise farmers fairer prices; how much they help is debated.
exercise: What is obsidian? | A sharp volcanic glass | A kind of dark red spice | A small silver Roman coin | A trading ship
exercise: How old is long-distance trade in obsidian? | Over 10,000 years | About 100 years | About 500 years | It never happened
exercise: What was the Silk Road really? | Many routes linking China to the Middle East and Europe | One long paved road that merchants walked from end to end | A railway line from Beijing to Rome built in the 1800s | A river along which silk was floated down to the sea
exercise: When was the name «Silk Road» made up? | In 1877 | In 500 BCE | In 1324 | In 2001
exercise: Who ruled the Mali Empire and went to Mecca in 1324? | Mansa Musa | Akbar the Great | Cyrus the Great of Persia | King Hammurabi
exercise: What did West Africa trade for salt across the Sahara? | Gold | Blocks of ice | Plastic beads | Silk carpets
exercise: Which West African city became a rich centre of learning? | Timbuktu | Lübeck, by the sea | Bergen, in the north | Kilwa
exercise: What did Indian Ocean sailors use to time their voyages? | The seasonal monsoon winds | Steam engines and coal | Printed railway timetables | Only the stars in winter
exercise: Which Swahili city grew rich from ocean trade? | Kilwa | Novgorod | Lübeck | Timbuktu
exercise: What was the Hanseatic League? | A group of trading towns around the Baltic and North Seas | A football league for the towns of northern Germany | An army of knights who fought in the Holy Land | A league of kings in Africa who traded gold and salt by camel
exercise: When was the Grand Bazaar in Istanbul started? | In the 1450s | In the 1950s | In 3100 BCE | In 1877
exercise: Were spices really worth their weight in gold? | Not usually; dear, but not that much | Yes, always exactly, gram for gram | Yes, they were worth even more than diamonds | No, spices were given away free
exercise: What did David Ricardo explain in 1817? | Both countries can gain by trading what they do best | Trade always makes one side lose what the other wins | Countries should never trade, but make everything | Gold is worth nothing, so only swap goods
exercise: Why are shipping containers all the same size? | So they load fast and cheap on any ship, train or lorry | Because of a royal law about boxes made back in 1066 | Because only one factory in the world makes the boxes | So they float better if they fall off the ship
exercise: How does most world trade travel today? | By sea | By plane | By camel | By post
exercise: Why can a T-shirt have several homes? | Cotton, cloth, sewing and selling may be in different countries | It is always made from start to finish in one small shop in one town | It grows on cotton trees already sewn together | It is made on the ship while it sails
exercise: What does the Fairtrade label aim to do? | Give farmers fairer prices | Make chocolate free for children | Ban all trade with poor countries | Make shops bigger
test: Leo asks how obsidian got hundreds of km before cars. What does it show? | People traded long before cities | It flew there all by itself in the wind | It was made in a factory just nearby | The Romans drove it there in carts
test: «silk road = one long motorway» says Mei's mate. Mei: nah, that's cap. Why? | It was many routes, with goods passed hand to hand | It was a sea route only, with no roads on land at all | It was one road, but with petrol stations every few km | It went only round Rome and never reached China
test: Amina says Mansa Musa was the richest man ever, innit. What do we know for sure? | He was famously rich; his gold reportedly lowered its price in Cairo | Nobody has ever heard of him outside Mali, so we know nothing | He was actually poor and borrowed all his gold from Egypt | He lived in 2020 and his money is in a bank
test: Hassan's uncle says his ancestors sailed with the monsoon winds. What would happen if a sailor ignored the season? | The winds would blow against him | The whole sea would freeze over | He would arrive there much faster | Nothing at all would happen
test: Finn asks why Lübeck is so fancy. What is the best answer? | Rich merchants of the Hanseatic League built it | A dragon left a big pile of gold there | It was the Roman capital of all Germany and Denmark | It was all built last year for tourists
test: Zeynep says the Grand Bazaar is older than America as a country. Right? | Yes, it was started in the 1450s, before the US existed | No, it only opened in 1990, so it's younger | No, it's a modern shopping mall built in 2000 | Yes, it is about 10,000 years old, older than cities
test: Charlie reads that spices hid rotten meat. What would his mum say? | People who could buy spices could buy fresh meat too | Yes, spices made rotten meat totally safe and tasty to eat | Spices were only for medicine, never for cooking | Meat never went off in the past, so it's a myth
test: Josh has loads of Messi, Leo loads of Ronaldo. What happens if they swap? | Both can end up happier | Only Josh wins | Only Leo wins | Both always lose
test: In EU4 trade just flows between nodes on the map. In real life, how do most goods move today? | In standard containers on huge ships | On camels across the great deserts | In planes only, because they're fastest | In the pockets of travellers
test: Mia's T-shirt says Made in Bangladesh, but the cotton came from India. What is true? | One thing can be made in several countries | Labels are always wrong about where things come from | Cotton grows only in Bangladesh, so the label is a lie | Bangladesh did nothing to it at all
test: Oliver asks if Fairtrade fixes everything. What is fair to say? | It helps some farmers, and how much is debated | It solves all the problems of poor farmers for good | It makes all chocolate free for everyone | It is a kind of football sticker
exam: Identify the substance traded over long distances more than ten thousand years ago, prior to the emergence of cities. | Obsidian, a volcanic glass | Porcelain from China | Plastic goods | Paper money
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called Silk Road? | A network of routes along which goods passed through many hands | A single paved highway travelled end to end by each merchant | A canal conveying silk from China to Rome by barge | A railway constructed in 1877 between Beijing and Venice
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Indian Ocean trade. | Voyages were timed in accordance with the seasonal monsoon winds | Voyages were possible only by steamship, from the nineteenth century | It commenced with European arrival in the twentieth century | It was confined to a single port on the coast of Arabia
exam: Ascertain the innovation introduced by Malcom McLean in 1956 that greatly reduced the cost of shipping. | The standard shipping container | The steam-powered cargo ship | The secret ballot for dock workers | The Fairtrade label
